{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 149,
    "dDurationMs": 6311,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Have you ever made ice cubes and then found\nthat when you take them out of the freezer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6460,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are spikes on them?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8740,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This phenomenon has caused a lot of curiosity\nand some concern."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13230,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The truth is, there is a simple physical process\nresponsible for ice cube spikes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17330,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ice cubs cool first from their top surface\nwhere they are in direct contact with the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20830,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cold freezer air and from which evaporative\ncooling can occur."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25300,
    "dDurationMs": 5389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the first ice crystals form on the surface\nof the ice cube and along its edges."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30689,
    "dDurationMs": 4511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now as that ice freezes, it expands by roughly\neight percent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35200,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That is due to hydrogen bonding between the\nwater molecules which makes them on average"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39660,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slightly further apart than they are in water."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42640,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So as the ice grows in from all sides, the\nwater in the middle has nowhere to go."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47300,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it is forced up and out through a hole\nin the ice."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51400,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These holes are often triangular in shape\nbecause ice crystals tend to meet at 60 degree"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55750,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "angles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56750,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the water comes up through the hole it\nfreezes around the edges forming a hollow"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61620,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spike."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62620,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And as the ice continues to grow, more water\nis forced up the spike, making it longer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67920,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This continues until all of the water is frozen,\nor until the end of the spike freezes shut."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73080,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you want to make ice cube spikes, they\ncan be created in most ordinary freezers and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77180,
    "dDurationMs": 2740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are three steps you can take to increase\nyour chances."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79920,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Number one, the ideal temperature seems to\nbe not too cold, between minus five and minus"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84750,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eight Celsius."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85750,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That is cold enough to freeze the water, but\nnot so quickly as to freeze off the tip of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90540,
    "dDurationMs": 1170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that ice cube spike."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91710,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Number two, distilled water seems to work\nbetter than tap water because even small concentrations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96740,
    "dDurationMs": 2030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of salt prevent spike formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98770,
    "dDurationMs": 5279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Number three, a fan in the freezer seems to\nhelp spike formation by increasing the circulation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104049,
    "dDurationMs": 2821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the air and increasing evaporative cooling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106870,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And given just the right conditions, very\nlarge ice spikes can form, for example, on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111810,
    "dDurationMs": 1210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bird baths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113020,
    "dDurationMs": 4599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In these cases the spikes are known as ice\nvases, for obvious reasons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117619,
    "dDurationMs": 4221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When looking at this structures, it is interesting\nto contemplate that they only form because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121840,
    "dDurationMs": 4819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of water’s unique property of expanding\nwhen it freezes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126659,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If water didn’t expand when it froze, the\nwhole earth would be dramatically different,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131569,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because, well, since ice is less dense, it\nforms on the surface and actually insulates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136209,
    "dDurationMs": 5081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the water underneath, so it remains liquid\nand allows life to survive in it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141290,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if, instead, ice were denser than water,\nit would sink to the bottom allowing for further"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146090,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cooling of that water and more ice formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149430,
    "dDurationMs": 6230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the ice would build up from the bottom\nupwards, giving less and less water for organisms"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155660,
    "dDurationMs": 5609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to live in, until, if cold conditions persisted\nfor long enough, all bodies of water would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161269,
    "dDurationMs": 5571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "completely solidify as ice, meaning that virtually\nno life could live in them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166840,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And at that point, the earth would also be\ncompletely white."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170930,
    "dDurationMs": 4959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it would reflect more of the sun’s light\ninto space, leading to further cooling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175889,
    "dDurationMs": 6451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if water didn’t expand when it froze,\nthe whole earth would be a cold, lifeless"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182340,
    "dDurationMs": 2369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "snowball."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184709,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it all depends on your starting conditions,\nbecause if there wasn’t very much ice on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189819,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the earth to begin with, it would all sink\nto the bottom of bodies of water, including"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194319,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arctic sea ice and that means the earth’s\nsurface would be less reflective, so it would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199269,
    "dDurationMs": 4711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "absorb more heat from the sun and it would\nget a lot warmer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203980,
    "dDurationMs": 6429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if ice didn’t float, if ice was denser\nthan water, either the earth would be much"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210409,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "colder than it is now or much hotter than\nit is now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215099,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in either case, life on earth might not\nexist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219469,
    "dDurationMs": 13850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if it did, we wouldn’t be able to make\nice cube spikes."
    } ]
  } ]
}
